1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:03,410
His lifeline snapped nearly a hundred metres under the North Sea.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:05,830
He had about five minutes of gas on his back.

3
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:08,350
North Sea, September 2012.

4
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:12,010
Chris Lemons, a saturation diver, was working on the seabed.

5
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:15,060
Above him, his support ship's positioning system failed.

6
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:16,810
In rough seas, it drifted.

7
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:20,820
His umbilical, carrying his gas and his radio, snagged on steel.

8
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:21,980
Then it parted.

9
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,320
In total darkness, he pulled himself back to the structure.

10
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,440
Then his emergency gas ran out.

11
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:30,400
It would take around half an hour to reach him.

12
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,560
When Dave Yuasa got there, Lemons was unresponsive.

13
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,570
He hauled him back into the diving bell.

14
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,210
There, Duncan Allcock gave him rescue breaths.

15
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,810
And Chris Lemons started breathing again.

16
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:42,120
The leading theory:

17
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:42,710
the cold.

18
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:46,610
Near-freezing water chilled him, slowing his brain's need for oxygen.

19
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:48,020
No one can prove it.

20
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:50,290
Three weeks later, he was back at work.
